---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "6051 160th St | A Pricing Analysis from Mark Gores";
const description = "A market-based pricing analysis prepared by Mark Gores for Steve Cody at 6051 160th St, Prior Lake.";

// Estimator defaults (recommended list price)
const LIST = 290000;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} noindex={true}>

<script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add('js');</script>

<nav class="proposal-nav">
  <a href="/" class="name">Mark Gores</a>
  <div class="nav-meta">Prepared for Steve Cody · July 2026</div>
</nav>

<main class="proposal">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="prop-hero">
    <div class="prop-hero-content">
      <div class="prop-label">A Pricing Analysis</div>
      <h1>For Steve Cody<br><em>6051 160th St, Prior Lake</em></h1>
      <p class="prop-hero-text">
        Steve, thanks for having me out. You asked for a realistic number to start from, so that's what this is. I price from a position of data, so everything here is built on sales you can check: what's sold on 160th Street, what the fixers in town are bringing, and what the duplex question is really worth. Here's the number, how I got to it, and what the sale would net the estate.
      </p>
      <div class="prop-signature">Mark</div>
    </div>

    <aside class="prop-facts">
      <div class="prop-facts-label">The property at a glance</div>
      <div class="facts-rows">
        <div class="facts-row"><span class="facts-v">1957</span><span class="facts-l">Rambler, main floor</span></div>
        <div class="facts-row"><span class="facts-v">3-car</span><span class="facts-l">Detached garage</span></div>
        <div class="facts-row"><span class="facts-v">0.45 ac</span><span class="facts-l">Lot</span></div>
        <div class="facts-row"><span class="facts-v">$350,000</span><span class="facts-l">County tax value</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="prop-facts-foot">
        A single-family rambler carved into three informal living spaces: the original three-bedroom home up, two makeshift units down. Newer windows, roof, furnace, AC, and septic work. Priced as what it legally is: a single-family home that needs work.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <!-- THE NUMBER -->
  <section class="recommendation">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Number</div>
    <h2>List at <em>$290,000</em></h2>
    <p class="rec-sub">
      Expected sale price: <strong>$280,000 to $305,000</strong><br>
      Most likely buyer: <strong>an investor, an owner-fixer, or a live-in landlord</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="rec-why">
      <p>
        Here's how I got there. The closest comp there is: 6125 160th, three doors down, same era, a comparable lot, a rental that sold at $315,000 needing an update. Your property needs more than an update, and the lot's east-only access costs it a little, so it sits below that. But the 0.45-acre lot and the detached three-car garage put it well above the small-lot fixers selling around town in the $240s and $280s. Priced at $290,000, it comes in under every livable single-family listing in town, which is what puts it in front of investors and owner-fixers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-range">
      <div class="range-card">
        <div class="range-label">Safe</div>
        <div class="range-price">$279,900</div>
        <div class="range-note">The certainty end. Draws the widest crowd of fixer buyers.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="range-card recommended">
        <div class="range-label">Recommended</div>
        <div class="range-price">$290,000</div>
        <div class="range-note">Where the comps, the lot, the garage, and the condition land when I put them together.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="range-card">
        <div class="range-label">Stretch</div>
        <div class="range-price">$305,000</div>
        <div class="range-note">Tests the top of the fixer band. Takes patience and the right investor who values the extra space.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-note">
      <div class="rec-note-label">A note on the tax value and a $250,000 starting point</div>
      <p>
        The county's $350,000 assumes a home in working condition; this one isn't there, so that figure isn't reachable as-is. A start closer to $250,000 goes the other way and hands a buyer the lot and the garage for free. $290,000 is the number I can defend line by line, to you, to the heirs, and to an attorney if it comes to that. The evidence for it is the rest of this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE PROPERTY -->
  <section class="specs">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Home</div>
    <h2>What's actually here.</h2>
    <figure class="specs-photo">
      <img src="/images/6051-160th-exterior.jpg" alt="6051 160th St, Prior Lake: rear of the home with the detached garage and driveway" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1200" />
      <figcaption>6051 160th St, from the rear: the detached three-car garage, the concrete drive, and the lower-level entrances.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="specs-grid">
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">1957</div><div class="spec-l">Year Built</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">Rambler</div><div class="spec-l">Style</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">3</div><div class="spec-l">Main-floor Beds</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">3/4</div><div class="spec-l">Main-floor Bath</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">3-car</div><div class="spec-l">Detached Garage</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">0.45 ac</div><div class="spec-l">Lot</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">3 spaces</div><div class="spec-l">Informal Units</div></div>
      <div class="spec"><div class="spec-v">$350,000</div><div class="spec-l">Tax Value</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="specs-narrative">
      The bones are a standard 1957 rambler: three bedrooms and a three-quarter bath on the main floor. The expensive things have been handled at some point, newer windows, roof, furnace, AC, and septic work, which is real money a buyer doesn't have to spend. The main-floor kitchen is a bright spot: newer cabinets and a new dishwasher, with the other appliances removed. The rest is a project: dated baths, worn flooring, and plumbing work in the lower level. The stairway down was floored over and turned into an upstairs laundry; it can be reopened to restore access to the lower level. Downstairs is the wrinkle: it's been split into a one-bedroom unit that needs real repair and a studio space with its own entrance but no bathroom. That layout is a selling point for the right buyer and a project for a conventional one, and the price reflects both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- THE COMPS -->
  <section class="comps">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Comps</div>
    <h2>Every recent sale on your street, plus the fixers.</h2>
    <p class="comps-intro">
      Five closed sales from NorthstarMLS. The top three are 160th Street itself, the strongest comps you can get because they share your exact location, the busy road included. The bottom two are the most recent needs-work sales in Prior Lake, which set the floor. Your property lives between them.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="comp-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Address</th>
            <th class="r">Sold</th>
            <th class="r">Date</th>
            <th class="r">SqFt</th>
            <th class="r">Lot</th>
            <th class="r">Yr</th>
            <th class="r">$/SF</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>6125 160th Street<br><span class="sub">Same street · needs update, ex-rental</span></td>
            <td class="r">$315,000</td>
            <td class="r">9/25/24</td>
            <td class="r">2,000</td>
            <td class="r">0.50</td>
            <td class="r">1960</td>
            <td class="r">$158</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>6151 160th Street<br><span class="sub">Same street · updated kitchen</span></td>
            <td class="r">$320,000</td>
            <td class="r">1/24/25</td>
            <td class="r">1,690</td>
            <td class="r">0.52</td>
            <td class="r">1960</td>
            <td class="r">$189</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>6225 160th Street<br><span class="sub">Same street · fully renovated, big lot</span></td>
            <td class="r">$475,000</td>
            <td class="r">6/10/25</td>
            <td class="r">3,231</td>
            <td class="r">1.20</td>
            <td class="r">1962</td>
            <td class="r">$147</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>4560 Dakota Avenue<br><span class="sub">Prior Lake · fixer, small lot</span></td>
            <td class="r">$287,000</td>
            <td class="r">12/08/25</td>
            <td class="r">1,753</td>
            <td class="r">0.26</td>
            <td class="r">1969</td>
            <td class="r">$164</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>16059 Cambridge Circle<br><span class="sub">Prior Lake · fixer, listed $180k, bid up</span></td>
            <td class="r">$241,000</td>
            <td class="r">10/03/25</td>
            <td class="r">1,650</td>
            <td class="r">0.27</td>
            <td class="r">1969</td>
            <td class="r">$146</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="summary">
            <td><strong>Median / Average</strong></td>
            <td class="r"><strong>$315k / $328k</strong></td>
            <td class="r">avg</td>
            <td class="r">2,065</td>
            <td class="r">0.55</td>
            <td class="r">1964</td>
            <td class="r"><strong>$161</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-label">Adjusted to your property</div>
      <p>
        6125 is the anchor: three doors down, same year, a comparable lot, a rental that sold at <strong>$315,000</strong> needing an update. Yours needs more work and carries the east-only access, so it comes in under 6125. But the 0.45-acre lot and the three-car garage lift it clearly above the small-lot fixers at $241k and $287k. Put all of it together and the number lands at <strong>$290,000</strong>. I listed and sold 6351 160th Street, a few doors up, back in 2012, so I know this stretch of road.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE DUPLEX QUESTION (dark) -->
  <section class="street-proof">
    <div class="street-inner">
      <div class="section-label-alt light">The Duplex Question</div>
      <h2>Why this is priced as a house, not a duplex.</h2>
      <p>
        It's a fair question, because two duplexes did sell in Prior Lake last year, and both brought $420,000. If your property were one of them, we'd be having a very different conversation. Here's why it isn't.
      </p>

      <div class="street-grid two">
        <div class="street-card">
          <div class="street-addr">6065 White Drive</div>
          <div class="street-sold">$420,000</div>
          <div class="street-meta">Side-by-side duplex · 2,860 sf<br>Built 1968 · a legal two-unit</div>
        </div>
        <div class="street-card">
          <div class="street-addr">4567 Colorado St SE</div>
          <div class="street-sold">$420,000</div>
          <div class="street-meta">Up/down duplex · 1,267 sf · sold in 1 day<br>Very well cared for and updated</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="street-takeaway">
        Both were legal, conforming, move-in two-units. Yours is zoned <strong>1A Residential Single Unit</strong>, the same as 6125 next door. The split into units is informal, not a permitted duplex, and one of the spaces has no bathroom. Making it a legal duplex would take a discretionary rezoning or variance from Credit River plus permits and code work, none of it something a buyer can bank on. So a lender and an appraiser both value this as a single-family home, which is why the $420k duplex sales don't set your price. The separate entrances stay in the listing as in-law or live-in-landlord potential for the right buyer. They just aren't a multiplier, and pricing on them would be pricing on a number the market won't pay.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CONDITION -->
  <section class="condition">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Condition</div>
    <h2>Straight about where it stands today.</h2>
    <p class="condition-intro">
      Most of the property is a project, and the number reflects it: dated baths, worn flooring, and a lower bathroom that needs plumbing work (a failing shower drain, and something worth a plumber's look at the toilet). The main-floor kitchen is the exception, with newer cabinets and a new dishwasher. Buyers price visible problems harder than a seller would, so $290,000 already accounts for the work.
    </p>
    <div class="condition-grid">
      <div class="condition-card">
        <div class="condition-tag">On cleaning</div>
        <p>Skip the renovation, but a basic clean-out and broom clean is worth the $1,000 to $3,000. A cluttered space pushes buyers toward flipper math and low offers; cleared and clean, the same house lets a higher-paying owner-fixer see the lot, the garage, and the potential. You're not spending to improve it, you're spending to reach the buyer who pays the most.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="condition-card">
        <div class="condition-tag">On the units</div>
        <p>The informal three-space layout is a genuine draw for a multi-gen family, a live-in landlord, or someone who wants to rent the lower level. We market it as potential, with the zoning up front, so the right buyer finds it without anyone overpromising what it legally is.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="condition-gallery">
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/6051-160th-kitchen-main.jpg" alt="Main-floor kitchen with newer wood cabinets" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Main-floor kitchen. Newer cabinets, new dishwasher; other appliances removed.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/6051-160th-bath-main.jpg" alt="Main-floor three-quarter bathroom" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Main-floor three-quarter bath. Dated, functional.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/6051-160th-lower-onebed.jpg" alt="Lower-level one-bedroom unit, kitchen and living area" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Lower level. The one-bedroom unit, kitchen and living space.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/6051-160th-lower-shower.jpg" alt="Lower-level tiled shower" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Lower-level shower. The drain needs plumbing work.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/6051-160th-lower-bath.jpg" alt="Lower-level bathroom" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Lower-level bathroom.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MARKET FLOOR / ACTIVES -->
  <section class="actives">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Competition</div>
    <h2>There's nothing cheap to compete with.</h2>
    <p class="actives-intro">
      Right now in Prior Lake, the least expensive single-family home you can actually move into is listed at <strong>$315,000</strong>, and it's already pending. Everything else livable starts higher:
    </p>
    <ul class="actives-list">
      <li><strong>$315,000</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; the cheapest livable resale in town. A 1958 home, small lot. <span class="dom-tag">under contract</span></li>
      <li><strong>$360,000+</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; where the rest of the move-in-ready resales begin, most on smaller lots than yours. <span class="dom-tag">active</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="actives-takeaway">
      Price a fixer with a 0.45-acre lot and a three-car garage at $290,000 and it becomes the cheapest single-family door into Prior Lake by a wide margin. That same scarcity took 16059 Cambridge from a $180k list to a $241k sale.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- COST ESTIMATOR -->
  <section class="fees" id="net">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Net to the Estate</div>
    <h2>What the sale actually clears.</h2>
    <p class="fees-intro">
      My listing fee is <strong>2.7%</strong>, published on my <a href="/how-i-sell">marketing plan page</a>, and I'd plan on the same 2.7% for the buyer's agent, which stays negotiable. The estimator below runs the full cost of the sale at any number, on Trademark Title's fee schedule, so the estate can see the net before anything is committed. It's set to the recommended $290,000; drag it to test other prices.
    </p>

    <div class="calc" id="calc">
      <div class="calc-label">Estimated net to the estate</div>

      <div class="calc-live" id="calc-live" hidden>
        <label class="calc-input-wrap" for="calc-price">
          <span class="calc-input-label">Sale price</span>
          <input type="text" id="calc-price" inputmode="numeric" value="$290,000" autocomplete="off" />
        </label>
        <input type="range" id="calc-slider" min="150000" max="450000" step="2500" value="290000" aria-label="Sale price slider" />
        <div class="calc-rows">
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Listing fee (2.7%)</div><div class="mono" id="calc-listing">$7,830</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Buyer's agent compensation (2.7%)</div><div class="mono" id="calc-buyer">$7,830</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Minnesota state deed tax (0.33%)</div><div class="mono" id="calc-deed">$957</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Settlement fee</div><div class="mono">$595</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Conservation fee</div><div class="mono">$5</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row"><div>Transaction fee</div><div class="mono">$625</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row calc-final"><div>Total cost to sell</div><div class="mono" id="calc-total">$17,842</div></div>
          <div class="calc-row calc-clear"><div>Net to the estate, before loan payoff &amp; prorated taxes</div><div class="mono" id="calc-clear">$272,158</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="calc-static" id="calc-static">
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="fee-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Sale price</th>
                <th class="r">Listing 2.7%</th>
                <th class="r">Buyer 2.7%</th>
                <th class="r">Deed tax + closing</th>
                <th class="r">Total cost</th>
                <th class="r">Net to estate</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>$280,000</td><td class="r">$7,560</td><td class="r">$7,560</td><td class="r">$2,149</td><td class="r">$17,269</td><td class="r">$262,731</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$290,000</td><td class="r">$7,830</td><td class="r">$7,830</td><td class="r">$2,182</td><td class="r">$17,842</td><td class="r">$272,158</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$305,000</td><td class="r">$8,235</td><td class="r">$8,235</td><td class="r">$2,232</td><td class="r">$18,702</td><td class="r">$286,298</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="calc-note">These are Trademark Title's actual seven-county-metro fees, settlement $595, conservation $5, transaction $625, plus Minnesota's 0.33% state deed tax. It runs the same math as Trademark's own net-sheet tool, so this figure lines up with what their calculator returns at the same price. It leaves out any loan payoff and prorated property taxes, which are specific to the closing, and an estate sale can add county recording fees around $46 per document, probate paperwork included. Trademark's net sheet at closing is the exact number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I'D SELL IT -->
  <section class="approach">
    <div class="section-label-alt">The Plan</div>
    <h2>How I'd sell it.</h2>
    <p class="approach-intro">
      The full marketing plan, the fee, the pricing evidence, and the weekly reporting I run on every listing all live on one page you can read and check. For this property specifically, the plan bends toward the buyer it actually has:
    </p>
    <ul class="approach-list">
      <li><strong>Aim at investors and owner-fixers,</strong> not the retail buyer the road and the condition scare off. That's the pool that pays for a property like this.</li>
      <li><strong>Market the condition and zoning up front,</strong> so the right buyer self-selects and the estate stays clear of disclosure trouble.</li>
      <li><strong>Weekly written reporting</strong> so you, and anyone advising the estate, always have showings, feedback, and my read. Useful when the family wants to see progress.</li>
    </ul>
    <a class="portal-link" href="/how-i-sell">
      <span class="portal-arrow">→</span>
      <span>
        <span class="portal-title">Read the full marketing plan</span>
        <span class="portal-sub">The complete plan, the pricing evidence behind every number, and the published 2.7% fee. The same page any of my sellers gets.</span>
      </span>
    </a>
  </section>

  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="closing">
    <div class="closing-inner">
      <div class="section-label-alt light">What's Next</div>
      <h2>A number you can stand behind.</h2>
      <p>
        I know the estate has moving parts, and that a clean, defensible number makes your job easier when several people are weighing in. That's what $290,000 is: not the highest figure I could float to win the listing, the one the sales support. If it helps to have the comps in writing to share with the family or an attorney, say the word and I'll send them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whenever you're ready, I can get you a Trademark Title net sheet for the exact number, and move on photography and prep quickly. The next step is yours.
      </p>

      <div class="closing-contact">
        <div class="closing-method">
          <span class="label">Call or Text</span>
          <a href="tel:+16122015447">612-201-5447</a>
        </div>
        <div class="closing-method">
          <span class="label">Email</span>
          <a href="mailto:mark@markgores.com">mark@markgores.com</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="closing-sign">Mark</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer class="prop-footer">
  <p>&copy; 2026 Mark Gores · RE/MAX Advantage Plus · Prior Lake, MN</p>
  <p class="footer-disclaimer">
    This pricing opinion is a good-faith estimate based on comparable sales from NorthstarMLS as of July 2026. It is not an appraisal. Final sale price depends on market conditions at the time of listing, buyer demand, property condition, and presentation. Zoning and legal-use notes are based on county records and should be independently verified with the City of Credit River. Information deemed reliable but not guaranteed.
  </p>
